// `print` and `println`, and `eprint` and `eprintln` beside them, whose format
// strings are Rust's.
//
//     println("x is {}", &[&x])
//     println("{:>8.3} {:#x}", &[&ratio, &mask])
//     eprintln("cannot read {}", &[&name])
//
// The `e` ones write to the error stream and are otherwise the same fn: what a
// line says and where it goes are two questions, and the second is answered by
// which of the four names is written.
//
// What a `{}` may say is what `println!` lets it say -- the argument by number,
// a fill and an alignment, a sign, `#`, a zero flag, a width, a precision, and
// a kind of formatting -- and it means the same thing here, because the same
// grammar is parsed and the value is written out by the `Display`, `Debug`,
// `LowerHex` and the rest that Rust already has. `runtime/src/fmt.rs` is the
// other half of this file and says how, and what a bad format string does.
//
// **The arity is not in the name and the kind is not at the call.** Both of
// those were the same missing thing, and §8 named it: "traits with code behind
// them". There are traits with code behind them now. So `print` through
// `print4` are one `print` taking a view -- one argument however many things
// are in it -- and `int(x)` and `text(s)` are gone, a value being asked what it
// looks like rather than told at the call.

// ---- Where a value writes itself --------------------------------------------

// The runtime's, and opaque here.
//
// What crosses is the address of one, and the five routines below are the only
// things that may be handed it: a `Show` body says *what* it is writing and
// never how, so nothing in this language had to learn how a float is spelled.
//
// The field is never read. A struct with no fields is not a type this language
// has, and this one stands for the whole of what the runtime keeps behind the
// address -- a buffer, what the `{}` asked for, and what has gone wrong.
pub struct Sink {
    held: ptr u8,
}

%symbol("__rt_show_int")
fn put_int(into: &Sink, n: i64): null;

%symbol("__rt_show_uint")
fn put_uint(into: &Sink, n: u64): null;

%symbol("__rt_show_real")
fn put_real(into: &Sink, x: f64): null;

%symbol("__rt_show_truth")
fn put_truth(into: &Sink, v: bool): null;

%symbol("__rt_show_text")
pub fn put(into: &Sink, s: str): null;

// ---- What a value looks like when it is written down ------------------------

// One member, and it writes rather than gives back.
//
// **Why it writes.** What a value looks like is not one number and not one
// piece of text: a struct is a name, its fields, and the punctuation between
// them, and there is no single thing to hand back that says all of that. This
// used to give back an `Arg` -- a struct with a tag saying which of four fields
// meant anything -- which could say "the number 42" and could not say
// "Point { x: 3, y: 4 }" at all. So the value is handed somewhere to write and
// writes as many pieces as it is made of.
//
// **The spec goes to the first piece.** `{:x}` of a number says what that
// number looks like; said of a value made of several parts there is no one
// number for it to be about. The first piece takes what was asked for, the rest
// take what nobody asked about, and the runtime says something where the spec
// was particular and the value was not. Width and alignment are the room around
// the value rather than the value, and apply to the whole of what came out.
//
// **One impl per kind the runtime can write.** There are five, and more than
// five types, because every whole number narrower than an `i64` widens into one
// exactly: an `i32` is an int, a `u8` a uint, an `f32` a real. A widening loses
// nothing and needs no rule; it is a *narrowing* that would want one, which is
// why `i128` and `u128` are not here.
//
// Every body reads its receiver, which is a `&i64` standing where an `i64` is
// wanted (§3). That was the last of the four things a reference is said to
// stand for and the one that was not implemented, and it is why none of this
// could have been written before.
pub trait Show {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink)
}

impl Show for i8 {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_int(into, self as i64) }
}

impl Show for i16 {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_int(into, self as i64) }
}

impl Show for i32 {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_int(into, self as i64) }
}

impl Show for i64 {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_int(into, self) }
}

impl Show for u8 {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_uint(into, self as u64) }
}

impl Show for u16 {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_uint(into, self as u64) }
}

impl Show for u32 {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_uint(into, self as u64) }
}

impl Show for u64 {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_uint(into, self) }
}

impl Show for f32 {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_real(into, self as f64) }
}

impl Show for f64 {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_real(into, self) }
}

impl Show for bool {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put_truth(into, self) }
}

impl Show for str {
    fn show(&self, into: &Sink) { put(into, self) }
}

// ---- What crosses to the runtime --------------------------------------------

// One routine, and the number of arguments is the view's length rather than
// part of the name. `how` is the two flags: 1 for the newline, 2 for the error
// stream, so the four names below are the four numbers 0 to 3.
//
// The view is handed straight over. It is two words -- an address and a length
// -- and every aggregate crosses as the address of a copy, so what the runtime
// is given is the address of those two words. The `Arg` version took a pointer
// and a count instead, because it had a `Vec` of its own making to read them
// off; there is no `Vec` any more, the arguments arriving as a view already.
%symbol("__rt_print")
fn out(how: i64, f: str, args: &(&dyn Show)[]): null;

// ---- Printing ---------------------------------------------------------------

// Four routines where there were twenty.
//
//     println("x is {}", &[&x])
//     println("{} and {}", &[&name, &count])
//     println("no arguments here", &[])
//
// The `&[..]` is an array of trait objects, borrowed as a view -- one argument
// however many things are in it, and the whole reason the arity left the name.
//
// The format string is read even where nothing follows it, so that `{{` is a
// brace here as it is everywhere else and a stray `{}` is reported rather than
// printed.

pub fn print(f: str, args: &(&dyn Show)[]) {
    out(0, f, args)
}

pub fn println(f: str, args: &(&dyn Show)[]) {
    out(1, f, args)
}

pub fn eprint(f: str, args: &(&dyn Show)[]) {
    out(2, f, args)
}

pub fn eprintln(f: str, args: &(&dyn Show)[]) {
    out(3, f, args)
}
